Speaker 1: And then we're going to go on today by discussing.

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:18.990
Speaker 1: Can you confirm to me that what were.

00:01:22.590 --> 00:01:27.470
Speaker 1: Okay, so I'm just told that some of our branches are watching from around the world.

00:01:27.630 --> 00:01:29.230
Speaker 1: So say God bless you to them.

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:31.870
Speaker 1: Hallelujah.

00:01:32.350 --> 00:01:35.510
Speaker 1: So we're gonna go on by discussing something.

00:01:35.510 --> 00:01:44.630
Speaker 1: And the reason I want to talk about this again is because some of you have been sending questions, and we want to talk about it.

00:01:45.030 --> 00:01:49.990
Speaker 1: So on the subject of Orthodoxy, we want to discuss baptism, water baptism.

00:01:51.270 --> 00:01:53.670
Speaker 1: And the first thing you need to understand is this.

00:01:53.670 --> 00:02:03.990
Speaker 1: Anyone who is a student of church history knows that controversy on water baptism is not new.

00:02:05.830 --> 00:02:06.950
Speaker 1: It's not new at all.

00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:27.040
Speaker 1: There are disagreements on mode of baptism, on subject of baptism, that is, who is to be baptized on the purpose of baptism and the regenerative nature of baptism.

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:28.720
Speaker 1: Does baptism save.

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:33.920
Speaker 1: These are arguments that are long older than your grandfather.

00:02:36.010 --> 00:02:38.930
Speaker 1: And I want to start with the mode of baptism.

00:02:38.930 --> 00:02:44.250
Speaker 1: A lot of people don't know this, but there are disagreements on the mode of baptism.

00:02:46.330 --> 00:02:50.970
Speaker 1: Some denominations believe that baptism can be done by sprinkling.

00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:53.610
Speaker 1: How many of you were baptized by sprinkling?

00:02:53.770 --> 00:02:54.650
Speaker 1: Raise your hand.

00:02:55.050 --> 00:02:56.490
Speaker 1: Thank you for your honesty.

00:02:59.050 --> 00:03:03.340
Speaker 1: And then some denominations did baptism by pouring.

00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:08.060
Speaker 1: And essentially the difference is more water is used.

00:03:10.060 --> 00:03:11.420
Speaker 1: That's just the basic difference.

00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:24.780
Speaker 1: So essentially, you are made to stand still or sit, and then they carry a basin, and the priest will say, baptize you in the name of the Father, Son, and Holy Spirit, and then pour water on your head.

00:03:30.790 --> 00:03:35.350
Speaker 1: And then the third is baptism by immersion.

00:03:35.750 --> 00:03:39.390
Speaker 1: And even immersion, there are varying beliefs.

00:03:39.390 --> 00:03:41.990
Speaker 1: Some believe that you'll be immersed three times.

00:03:43.030 --> 00:03:45.430
Speaker 1: How many of you were immersed but immersed three times?

00:03:46.070 --> 00:03:59.460
Speaker 1: All right, So like I said, even when it comes to the mode of baptism, there are different understandings.

00:03:59.460 --> 00:03:59.860
Speaker 1: Come on.

00:03:59.860 --> 00:04:00.780
Speaker 1: Are we clear on that?

00:04:02.460 --> 00:04:05.060
Speaker 1: So I think that's a good place for me to start.

00:04:05.060 --> 00:04:06.060
Speaker 1: I just want to say.

00:04:06.700 --> 00:04:11.980
Speaker 1: So what then does the Bible say about this?

00:04:14.300 --> 00:04:18.700
Speaker 1: I think there are stronger arguments for baptism by immersion.

00:04:20.039 --> 00:04:22.199
Speaker 1: First and foremost, the linguistic argument.

00:04:22.199 --> 00:04:23.519
Speaker 1: The linguistic argument.

00:04:23.519 --> 00:04:28.919
Speaker 1: And simply what I mean by that is the choice of word, the fact that the Bible calls it baptism.

00:04:28.919 --> 00:04:35.159
Speaker 1: Greek word baptizo, which means to immerse, to deep or to submerge.

00:04:36.199 --> 00:04:37.239
Speaker 1: Are you following me?

00:04:37.719 --> 00:04:42.439
Speaker 1: So just by the choice of words, and bear in mind there was a different Greek word for sprinkling.

00:04:44.610 --> 00:04:49.250
Speaker 1: And so if sprinkling was what was intended, they would have used that Greek word.

00:04:50.850 --> 00:04:53.570
Speaker 1: Rantizo is the Greek word for sprinkling.

00:04:54.610 --> 00:04:58.690
Speaker 1: So instead of rantizo, what was used was baptizo.

00:05:02.370 --> 00:05:06.610
Speaker 1: And that suggests full body immersion.

00:05:06.610 --> 00:05:07.210
Speaker 1: Immersion.

00:05:07.210 --> 00:05:24.480
Speaker 1: And even amongst sects that believe that sprinkling was okay, they actually thought that water baptism by immersion was more ideal, save for the fact that water was scarce.

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:27.159
Speaker 1: Water was scarce.

00:05:27.159 --> 00:05:55.610
Speaker 1: So even amongst people who had a more liberal understanding on how baptism could be done, some thought it was not ideal, except in special cases where for the sake of weather, if you are living in Antarctica and you want to, you know, so for case of weather or health or scarcity of water, then you can make an exception.

00:05:58.890 --> 00:06:04.360
Speaker 1: The second argument for water baptism by immersion is the biblical description.

00:06:04.430 --> 00:06:07.150
Speaker 1: Description of how water baptism was to be done.

00:06:07.950 --> 00:06:09.310
Speaker 1: The biblical description.

00:06:09.310 --> 00:06:11.870
Speaker 1: And I want to just share three, two quick texts.

00:06:11.870 --> 00:06:16.150
Speaker 1: Matthew chapter 3, verse 16 and Acts chapter 8, verse 38 and 39.

00:06:16.150 --> 00:06:16.830
Speaker 1: I'll take that again.

00:06:16.830 --> 00:06:22.350
Speaker 1: Matthew, chapter 3, verse 16 and Acts Chapter 8, verse 38 to 39.

00:06:24.590 --> 00:06:27.710
Speaker 1: Matthew 3:16 tells us something about Jesus's baptism.

00:06:27.790 --> 00:06:33.630
Speaker 1: He says, and when he had been baptized, Jesus came up immediately from the water.

00:06:34.190 --> 00:06:35.230
Speaker 1: Jesus did what?

00:06:36.910 --> 00:06:40.350
Speaker 1: And so this suggests full immersion.

00:06:42.910 --> 00:06:44.030
Speaker 1: Come on, are you with me?

00:06:44.590 --> 00:06:55.070
Speaker 1: If the Bible says he came up immediately, it suggests or implies full immersion, that he went down fully into the water and came up out of it.

00:06:55.790 --> 00:07:05.530
Speaker 1: The Bible says about the Ethiopian eunuch, Acts 8, 38, they went down into the water, Philip and the eunuch, and he baptized him.

00:07:05.530 --> 00:07:10.650
Speaker 1: And when they came up out of the water, it was a full immersion.

00:07:16.330 --> 00:07:24.490
Speaker 1: And then the third argument that is often given is based off of the choice of words describing John's baptism.

00:07:24.810 --> 00:07:27.770
Speaker 1: I want you to see it for yourself in John, chapter 3, verse 23.

00:07:28.090 --> 00:07:43.770
Speaker 1: John chapter 3, verse 23, John chapter 3, verse 23, it says now John also was baptizing in Aenon near Salim, because there was what much water there?

00:07:46.810 --> 00:07:56.390
Speaker 1: So the need for much water or him choosing to stayed there because there was much water implied that a full immersion was required.

00:07:57.190 --> 00:07:58.630
Speaker 1: Amen, somebody.

00:07:59.830 --> 00:08:05.110
Speaker 1: And then the fourth, which is for me the most important, is theological symbolism.

00:08:05.350 --> 00:08:07.110
Speaker 1: The theological symbolism.

00:08:07.350 --> 00:08:14.710
Speaker 1: If water baptism is supposed to symbolize that you are buried with Christ and that you are raised up with him.

00:08:16.800 --> 00:08:24.400
Speaker 1: It therefore follows that a full immersion is more ideal.

00:08:28.480 --> 00:08:36.480
Speaker 1: Text like Colossians 2:12 that says having been buried with him in baptism, in which you were raised up with him.

00:08:36.799 --> 00:08:46.289
Speaker 1: All right, so understanding the typology there just gives you a suggestion what should be the preference or preferred mood.

00:08:49.649 --> 00:09:02.129
Speaker 1: But listen, as simple as what I just shared is you can line up brilliant theologians on this stage and they will give you strong arguments.

00:09:03.569 --> 00:09:14.360
Speaker 1: And the reason I want to do this is because you see a lot of especially young people join their mouth to arguments that they don't really know much about.

00:09:15.800 --> 00:09:19.160
Speaker 1: So there is usually a huge context gap.

00:09:20.360 --> 00:09:22.200
Speaker 1: You don't even know what is happening.

00:09:23.240 --> 00:09:29.160
Speaker 1: So having talked about the different modes, now let us get into serious business.

00:09:29.720 --> 00:09:40.870
Speaker 1: I want to do a comparison of different denominations and tell you what they believe about baptism and if it is regenerative and who can be baptized.

00:09:41.510 --> 00:09:44.550
Speaker 1: I think I made a slide and let's see if I can flow with that.

00:09:44.550 --> 00:09:46.630
Speaker 1: I want to start with the Roman Catholic Church.

00:09:47.270 --> 00:09:53.230
Speaker 1: How many of you did not know that the Roman Catholic Church believed that baptism saves?

00:09:53.230 --> 00:09:54.630
Speaker 1: How many of you did not know that?

00:09:55.670 --> 00:09:56.950
Speaker 1: Thank you for your honesty.

00:09:57.910 --> 00:09:59.390
Speaker 1: Can I agree that this is.

00:09:59.390 --> 00:09:59.790
Speaker 1: Raise your hand.

00:09:59.790 --> 00:10:00.190
Speaker 1: Raise your hand.

00:10:00.190 --> 00:10:00.790
Speaker 1: Well, raise your hand.

00:10:00.790 --> 00:10:01.270
Speaker 1: Thank you.

00:10:01.420 --> 00:10:05.460
Speaker 1: Can I agree that this is 50% or 40%?

00:10:05.460 --> 00:10:06.500
Speaker 1: Raise it up so we can see.

00:10:06.500 --> 00:10:07.380
Speaker 1: Let's do a census.

00:10:07.380 --> 00:10:08.700
Speaker 1: How many of you did not know that?

00:10:11.500 --> 00:10:12.540
Speaker 1: Almost 50%.

00:10:14.700 --> 00:10:15.940
Speaker 1: So here is what you needed.

00:10:15.940 --> 00:10:16.780
Speaker 1: You need to know.

00:10:17.500 --> 00:10:17.860
Speaker 1: Yes.

00:10:17.860 --> 00:10:20.380
Speaker 1: They believe that baptism is regenerative.

00:10:22.780 --> 00:10:26.540
Speaker 1: They believe that it is sacramental, meaning it imparts grace.

00:10:26.540 --> 00:10:27.100
Speaker 1: Listen.

00:10:27.320 --> 00:10:30.840
Speaker 1: They believe that baptism washes away original sin.

00:10:33.400 --> 00:10:34.680
Speaker 1: Did you hear what I said?

00:10:35.480 --> 00:10:47.480
Speaker 1: They believe that it washes away original sin and that it imparts sanctifying grace and that it incorporates one into Christ and his church.

00:10:48.280 --> 00:10:55.810
Speaker 1: That water baptism incorporates you into Christ and his Church.

00:11:00.210 --> 00:11:05.490
Speaker 1: And for avoidance of doubts, I want to quote from their Catechism 1257.

00:11:05.490 --> 00:11:14.850
Speaker 1: He said the Church does not know of any other means other than baptism that assures entry into the eternal Beatitudes.

00:11:15.010 --> 00:11:17.010
Speaker 1: He says they don't know of any other means.

00:11:20.060 --> 00:11:21.020
Speaker 1: Are you following this?

00:11:25.580 --> 00:11:28.220
Speaker 1: Next, did they believe infants baptism?

00:11:28.540 --> 00:11:29.020
Speaker 1: Yes.

00:11:31.580 --> 00:11:39.820
Speaker 1: They believe that infants inherit original sin and baptism removes disdain and brings them into grace.

00:11:44.150 --> 00:11:45.030
Speaker 1: Are you following this?

00:11:48.790 --> 00:11:51.030
Speaker 1: The believes they believe.

00:11:51.350 --> 00:11:52.550
Speaker 1: What English is that?

00:11:53.750 --> 00:11:57.670
Speaker 1: That's past participle, plural, continuous.

00:12:02.790 --> 00:12:05.590
Speaker 1: And they believe that the child grows in grace as they mature.

00:12:07.350 --> 00:12:12.310
Speaker 1: Quoting from the Catechism CCC 1250, it says, born with a fallen nature.

00:12:12.550 --> 00:12:17.990
Speaker 1: They also need the new birth in baptism.

00:12:18.230 --> 00:12:19.430
Speaker 1: Look at the choice of words.

00:12:19.510 --> 00:12:21.430
Speaker 1: The new birth in baptism.

00:12:24.070 --> 00:12:27.110
Speaker 1: Now, what did early church fathers think about this?

00:12:27.830 --> 00:12:29.830
Speaker 1: And I'm quoting this for a reason.

00:12:31.510 --> 00:12:32.950
Speaker 1: Let me start with origin.

00:12:34.070 --> 00:12:35.430
Speaker 1: AD230.

00:12:36.160 --> 00:12:42.640
Speaker 1: He said the Church received from the apostles the tradition of giving baptism even to infants.

00:12:48.080 --> 00:12:49.440
Speaker 1: Did you hear what I just said?

00:12:50.640 --> 00:12:51.920
Speaker 1: Who did I just quote?

00:12:54.560 --> 00:12:55.840
Speaker 1: An early church father.

00:12:56.800 --> 00:12:58.880
Speaker 1: Is 230 A.D. early enough?

00:13:00.080 --> 00:13:00.880
Speaker 1: Yes, it is.

00:13:04.730 --> 00:13:06.890
Speaker 1: Some of you already know why I'm stressing this.

00:13:07.050 --> 00:13:09.930
Speaker 1: But for those of you who don't know, I will come back to this.

00:13:14.010 --> 00:13:15.450
Speaker 1: Cyprian of Carthage.

00:13:16.170 --> 00:13:19.290
Speaker 1: 250 A.D. he said, affirmed.

00:13:19.370 --> 00:13:22.890
Speaker 1: He affirmed that baptism should not be delayed even to the eighth day.

00:13:24.170 --> 00:13:25.050
Speaker 1: Don't even wait.

00:13:25.050 --> 00:13:25.930
Speaker 1: To the eighth day.

00:13:27.060 --> 00:13:29.060
Speaker 1: Baptized the child instantly.

00:13:30.180 --> 00:13:39.940
Speaker 1: And by the way, in case you were wondering how we came about baptism by sprinkling, when the Bible is very clear, is by immersion.

00:13:40.660 --> 00:13:41.220
Speaker 1: It was.

00:13:42.180 --> 00:13:50.180
Speaker 1: It was a new theology to accommodate what I will argue to be a new invention of infant baptism.

00:13:51.860 --> 00:13:57.320
Speaker 1: And so because they thought it's unsafe to immerse children, they had to come up with a new tradition.

00:14:01.480 --> 00:14:02.680
Speaker 1: Are you following this?

00:14:03.560 --> 00:14:09.320
Speaker 1: I'm helping you see how the goal post can be moved gradually.

00:14:15.000 --> 00:14:20.840
Speaker 1: And in case you have noticed already, I'm helping you see how final authority must be the Bible itself.

00:14:24.240 --> 00:14:26.480
Speaker 1: Because this I'm quoting early church fathers.

00:14:26.480 --> 00:14:46.640
Speaker 1: Irenaeus, 180 AD he came to save all through himself, all who through him are reborn in God.

00:14:47.100 --> 00:14:51.340
Speaker 1: Infants, children, youths and elders.

00:14:51.900 --> 00:14:55.020
Speaker 1: In his book Against Heresies, ironic.
